rawer somewhere because it  would require  a ton of moving parts and union  league so much more is there a bubble  plan somewhere in somebody's top right  hand drawer to pull out if necessary  you find it necessary well we've  discussed bubble scenarios all the way  back into the summer when we in the  union together looked at that i i think  i would just tell you a couple of things  about a bubble there's no absolute  bubble you've always got people coming  in and out not players and coaches but  you know service workers support workers  and things like that and that can  introduce the virus  and being in a bubble does not change  the basic need for all the other parts  of the protocol  if you're in a bubble you still must do  mask you still must do distance you  still must do hand hygiene you still  have to change all of these things so  it doesn't change anything on our  day-to-day operations the other thing i  think you have to consider there though  rich is not just the infectious disease  but what's the mental health and the  emotional behavioral health toll inside  a bubble  when you're asking people to leave their  families and isolate themselves for  months at a time  that's a substantial challenge for  michelin behavioral health  and i think that's something that  doesn't always get discussed but i think  it's an important part of the  calculation so you're always looking at  what can you gain  and versus what is the what is the  downside so i would just say we have all  those discussions we continue to  evaluate  but our focus right now is is on the  protocols which largely have been  working  and trying to make sure we're all  following them as carefully as we can  and then the last thing that you  mentioned that you know  in a team he takes all 11 right to  matriculate a ball down the field or all  11 to stop  a team from doing that and you know if  one person doesn't do the job then  that's a problem  uh you know not to call anybody out but  in the two